          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            A
            %em certification
            or
            %em formal verification
            of a computer program is a formalized guarantee – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            – that the program has given specified properties. For instance, it could be guaranteed to compute a given output based on a given input, or to always terminate, or to not include a certain kind of security hole.
          %p
            Certifications often take the form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            (whence “formal methods”
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#WLBF09"} WLBF09
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            of some sort (under
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programs+as+proofs"} programs as proofs
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            based on highly expressive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            (including
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
          %p
            It is also possible to write a program in some less strongly typed language and provide an “external” certification for it, rather than one built into the program itself. Computer proof assistants like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
            are also used for this, using a formal representation of some other programming language. There are also other program analysis tools which can produce automated proofs of certain aspects of a computer program, such as safety and termination (although of course a
            %em complete
            solution to termination-checking is impossible, being the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/halting+problem"} halting problem
          %h3#via_homotopy_type_theory Via homotopy type theory
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#GhaniEtAl15"} Ghani et al. 15
          %blockquote
            %p The cost of software failure is truly staggering. Well known individual cases include the Mars Climate Orbiter failure (£80 million), Ariane Rocket disaster (£350 million), Pentium Chip Division failure (£300 million), and more recently the heartbleed bug (est. £400 million). There are many, many more examples. Even worse, failures such as one in the Patriot Missile System and another in the Therac-25 radiation system have cost lives. More generally, a 2008 study by the US government estimated that faulty software costs the US economy £100 billion annually.
            %p
              There are many successful approaches to software verification (testing, model checking etc). One approach is to find mathematical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
              that guarantees of software correctness. However, the complexity of modern software means that hand-written mathematical proofs can be untrustworthy and this has led to a growing desire for computer-checked proofs of software correctness. Programming languages and interactive proof systems like
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NuPRL"} NuPRL
              and Idris have been developed based on a formal system called
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %p
              In this way, both large mathematical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
              such as the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/four+color+theorem"} Four Colour Theorem
            %p
              This is related to the problem of reusability and modularity: a component in a software system should be replaceable by another which behaves the same way even though it may be constructed in a completely different way. That is, we need an “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"}> extensional equality
              ” which is computationally well behaved (that is, we want to run programs using this equality). Finding such an equality is a fundamental and difficult problem which has remained unresolved for over 40 years.
            %p#WeMightHaveASolution
              But now it looks like we might have a solution! Fields medalist
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
              has come up with a completely different take on the problem by thinking of equalities as paths such as those which occur in one of the most abstract branches of mathematics, namely
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} Homotopy Type Theory
              (HoTT). In HoTT, two objects are completely interchangeable if they behave the same way. However, most presentations of HoTT involve
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              which lack computational justification and, as a result, we do not have programming languages or verification systems based upon HoTT. The goal of our project is to fix that, thereby develop the first of a new breed of HoTT-based programming languages and verification systems, and develop case studies which demonstrate the power of HoTT to programmers and those interested in formal verification.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion+monad"} completion monad
              has been used to produce code for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
              computations with certified approximation bounds. See at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion+monad"} completion monad
              for more.
          %p (…)
